WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.830
With our physical material now set up,

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.269
the last thing that we're gonna do

3
00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:09.029
is put in the landscape visibility mask

4
00:00:09.029 --> 00:00:14.029
so that we can have holes inside of our landscape

5
00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:17.190
to allow any users to go underneath our landscape.

6
00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.750
While we are not necessarily gonna be building

7
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.969
any elaborate cave systems or anything like that,

8
00:00:22.969 --> 00:00:25.710
this is just a really good thing to know about

9
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.910
in case your project does require

10
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.190
some type of cave system.

11
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.690
And here is an example on the screen

12
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.530
of what I'm talking about.

13
00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.689
Here we have just a normal area

14
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:35.049
and then we have the masked area.

15
00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:36.250
And what is actually happening

16
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.009
is we're actually painting this out

17
00:00:38.009 --> 00:00:40.609
and not only are we painting the visual data out,

18
00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:42.829
but we're also painting the collision.

19
00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:45.289
Now, this is actually fairly easy

20
00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:47.369
to set up inside of our material.

21
00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:50.170
All we have to do is change our material domain

22
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.770
from its defaults blend mode to masked.

23
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.590
And then inside of our opacity mask input,

24
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.109
we have a special node called landscape visibility mask.

25
00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:04.469
Now, if you want to get a little bit more advanced

26
00:01:04.469 --> 00:01:05.829
with your setup,

27
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:09.769
you can select each individual landscape tile.

28
00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.010
And there is this landscape whole material,

29
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.370
which will allow you to specify a specific material

30
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.950
instead of having to have a landscape visibility mask

31
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.650
for your entire landscape.

32
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.250
For our project here,

33
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.909
what we're gonna do is I'm gonna hit control space

34
00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:30.189
and I'm gonna open up our matte landscape.

35
00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:32.109
And so first things first,

36
00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.049
we're gonna come over here to our blend mode

37
00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:36.540
and we're gonna change this to mask.

38
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.859
And then I'm gonna zoom in here

39
00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:39.980
and over here we have our opacity mask.

40
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.900
So we're gonna look for our landscape

41
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.099
and we want our landscape visibility mask.

42
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:47.769
So right there.

43
00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:51.599
And then we're just gonna plug this in like so.

44
00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:55.599
Once that's done, let's make sure we apply this and save.

45
00:01:55.599 --> 00:01:57.239
And while that saves,

46
00:01:57.239 --> 00:01:59.840
the next thing that we need to do is in order to use this,

47
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.040
we have to come over to the sculpt section

48
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.840
of our landscape tools, click on visibility,

49
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.000
and then we can paint in our visibility

50
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.400
or hide our visibility

51
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.360
just like we're sculpting in landscape.

52
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.639
So let's take a look at that now.

53
00:02:13.639 --> 00:02:15.520
All right, I'm gonna go ahead and close this down

54
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.479
and then I'm gonna come up here to our selection,

55
00:02:17.479 --> 00:02:20.520
to our landscape, I'm gonna come over to sculpt

56
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.219
and then we're gonna look for our visibility,

57
00:02:22.219 --> 00:02:23.479
which is right here.

58
00:02:23.479 --> 00:02:26.199
And then make sure my brush isn't too large.

59
00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:28.080
And then I'm just gonna hold down the left mouse button

60
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.280
and you can see that I'm starting to paint in there.

61
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.740
And then if I hold down shift,

62
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.680
you can see that I've added that right back.

63
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.680
And let's just go ahead

64
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.219
and show you that we can actually go through this.

65
00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:40.479
We're just gonna play from right here.

66
00:02:40.479 --> 00:02:42.879
So check this out, just drop right off there.

67
00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:46.719
So it does create a hole that you can go inside of.

68
00:02:46.719 --> 00:02:50.199
And again, this is useful for anything, a cave system.

69
00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:53.560
If you want to have maybe a house

70
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.699
that is partially set inside of the landscape,

71
00:02:56.699 --> 00:03:00.819
or you've got maybe a very large building

72
00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:02.680
and you have sub floors

73
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.360
that need to go underneath the terrain

74
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.319
and you don't necessarily want to get rid

75
00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:08.259
of a square component or push it all the way down.

76
00:03:08.259 --> 00:03:13.259
This is a great way to get rid of those landscape areas,

77
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.759
both visually and remove the collision.
